{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1078065-why-do-you-like-simulationism","id":1078065,"title":"Why do you like simulationism?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-04-13T18:21:05-07:00","id":1078065,"title":"Why do you like simulationism?","content":"I'd like to discuss something. I have a slight feeling that the new editions of WoD games will move toward lighter rules and narrative style. I'm basing it on their background in nordic LARP and on the LARP games they organized so far. It's not a secret, that I'm not a big fan of narrative mechanics and I somewhat fear that it will turn out to be something like the 7th Sea 2nd edition in comparison to its first. But pondering on that is not really the goal here. <br /> <br /> WoD was and is always a more story-centered game. In a sense, I think it's approach paved the way to today's super-ruels-light, narrative games. At least I'm sure a lot of the creators of those games were influenced by it back in the day. WoD also has a fairly light ruleset in comparison to it's contemporary peers. Although, despite all that, it is a classic rpg in a sense that it has a fairly detailed ruleset and moderately simulationist mechanics, advancement tracks, characters defined by stats, not keywords and the lack of narrative rules, among other things. <br /> <br /> So, what I really want to talk about and hear your opinions, estimated dwellers in the dark:<br /> <br /> - What do you think, what are the merits of detailed rules and simulation? Not that it's better or not than the other side (I think it's a matter of personal taste), but what do you like in those kind of games, in comparison?<br /> <br /> - How can details enhance gameplay?<br /> <br /> - How simulation could add to the story?<br /> <br /> - How all these aspects are affecting the overall gaming experience?<br /> <br /> Disclaimer: I don't want it to derail to a &quot;what is the true way of gaming&quot; argument, I'm interested in a positive thread, to hear about other people's thoughts and experiences, please bear that in mind, thanks!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-14T20:24:52-07:00","id":1078733,"title":"","content":"The merits of detailed rules is that storytellers have the option to use or remove rules rather than having to make the rules up whole cloth (or to adjust the rules to fit their campaigns). Simulation games are also usually more balanced than narrative games because the designers of simulation games tend to test every aspect of their game (though there are always mistakes made because the designers are human). Details add options, which means that characters can be unique, while narrative games tend to make characters that tend to quite similar. The primary weakness of simulation games is that the storyteller must understand the system backwards-and-forwards to make sure that the games mover smoothly while narrative games can be run with only a basic understanding of the system.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-15T01:18:07-07:00","id":1078804,"title":"","content":"I emphasize narrative storytelling, actually, but suspension of disbelief is vital for a powerful narrative. To know that the NPC's have their own agendas and trajectories in the background, and will continue to follow them rather than just being there to support the PC's stories, is a beautiful thing. A well-defined environment is grounding and enriching. It will have a lot more surprises for the players. Narratives are often made from familiar tropes. A thoroughly-realized world can give rise to much more natural and unique scenarios--truth is stranger than fiction. You don't create truth in a roleplaying game, but you can simulate it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1503,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","user":"Faradn","date":". 04-15-2017, 05:01 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","name":"Faradn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1503_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"792"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-10T01:36:58-07:00","id":1090638,"title":"","content":"Simulationist systems seem to be better at providing <i>emergent</i> stories. Where a battle is won or lost <i>because of a nail</i>, as opposed to by fiat. They also support foreshadowing in a more naturalistic, less fiat-based manner. <br /> <br /> It is, of course, a matter of taste and preferences.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12622-vicky_molokh","name":"vicky_molokh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12622_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"75"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-10T11:55:02-07:00","id":1090808,"title":"","content":"Thank you all for sharing you thoughts, I'll write something in the not-so distant future too!<br /> <br /> In the meantime, I wanted to react to this:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>vicky_molokh</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1078065-why-do-you-like-simulationism?p=1090638#post1090638\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Simulationist systems seem to be better at providing <i>emergent</i> stories. Where a battle is won or lost <i>because of a nail</i>, as opposed to by fiat. They also support foreshadowing in a more naturalistic, less fiat-based manner.<br /> <br /> It is, of course, a matter of taste and preferences.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree. I always felt the same and that's one of the main reasons why I prefer simulationist games. The whole shaping of the story feels more natural, more organic and I feel more like &quot;living my character (even when as a ST through the NPCs)&quot; than collaboratively writing a story. I know for a lot of people that's the exact goal of playing rpgs, and it's somewhat is to me too, it's just a question of nuances and as you said, tastes and preferences. I won't say narrative games are &quot;forced&quot;, but their style of storytelling feels more artificial to me. Like something happens, because it's <i>dramatic, </i>not because it's the logical consequence of the events. I know it's not nearly as black and white, but that's the general feel I get. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-10T12:41:01-07:00","id":1090823,"title":"","content":"I use ST and DM interchangeable since I play a lot of D&amp;D in addition to WoD.<br /> <br /> I don't like rails. I appreciate that an ST can write a good story, but if I have little to no impact on the story other than playing a predetermined part I very quickly lose interest. I like narratives, but I don't like narrativism, where the system is designed to serve the plot. More often than not I find that extensive fiat causes stories to flounder than succeed. In a narrativist or rules light system it's often been the case that, say a character has +20 to pick locks, the the DM decides he needs a 30 because a chance of failure keeps it &quot;interesting&quot;, despite the character being a master lockpick and the lock being relatively simple. In a simulationist system you know approximately how good a character is in comparison to the world around them. How good does a character need to be to beat an Army private most of the time? How good do they have to be to hack into a personal computer? A corporate server? A military server? In many cases pure narrativism dictates that how good they are exactly does not matter, what matters is whether or not the events are &quot;interesting&quot;, where in a simulationist system you can be generally comfortable in knowledge that your character is good at things they're good at, and bad at things they're bad at. So I often see silly contrivances where a master lockpick breaks his picks on a plywood door with a garbage lock because the ST didn't think it would be interesting if the characters managed to get in or out of whatever scenario the door was enforcing.<br /> <br /> Some modern systems have taken this so far as to have &quot;Story&quot; or &quot;Fate&quot; points that a player can spend to summon plot points, which I am not a fan of. My preferred style has always been that the players control their characters, the ST controls the world around them and it's reactions. Simulationism fits that best. It's not the characters against the DM or the characters against the pamphlet of very linear notes the DM has written about the story, it's the characters against the world, and the consequences of their own actions.<br /> <br /> I will say that this is obviously my preference. Play RPGs however you and your players enjoy. I just raise an eyebrow when an ST tells me that the chronicle being run is inspired by a book that s/he wrote.<br /> <br /> And while I used the terms to respond to your post, I think GNS theory is garbage. I like &quot;simulationist&quot; systems, but I also value good roleplay and don't think it's something that can be mechanically compelled. And I'm &quot;Gamist&quot; in the sense that I think every character should be equally capable of contributing to the overall goals of the group.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11512,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11512-zanos","user":"Zanos","date":". 05-10-2017, 03:45 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11512-zanos","name":"Zanos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11512_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"550"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-10T13:28:24-07:00","id":1090841,"title":"","content":"I think the WOD should function on a what i call &quot;Cinematic SImulationism&quot; that is to say rather than attempting to say &quot;Its like the real world plus vampires&quot;, its easier to go &quot;Its like an action/horror/etc movie&quot; certain rules emulate tropes rather than 1 for 1 truths thus creating an emergent story that FEELS like a certain type of movie but doesn't have obvious Co-gming mechanics.<br /> <br /> Then with Degrees of Metacurrency addons depending on the group preferences with the least being Xp and the Most being Fate point style. This i think would give the best set of options all around. I don't think though any attempt at making &quot;WOD MORE REALISTIC&quot; will be anything but horrible bouts of saddness.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-10T13:33:25-07:00","id":1090845,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Zanos</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1078065-why-do-you-like-simulationism?p=1090823#post1090823\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I use ST and DM interchangeable since I play a lot of D&amp;D in addition to WoD.<br /> <br /> I don't like rails. I appreciate that an ST can write a good story, but if I have little to no impact on the story other than playing a predetermined part I very quickly lose interest. I like narratives, but I don't like narrativism, where the system is designed to serve the plot. More often than not I find that extensive fiat causes stories to flounder than succeed. In a narrativist or rules light system it's often been the case that, say a character has +20 to pick locks, the the DM decides he needs a 30 because a chance of failure keeps it &quot;interesting&quot;, despite the character being a master lockpick and the lock being relatively simple. In a simulationist system you know approximately how good a character is in comparison to the world around them. How good does a character need to be to beat an Army private most of the time? How good do they have to be to hack into a personal computer? A corporate server? A military server? In many cases pure narrativism dictates that how good they are exactly does not matter, what matters is whether or not the events are &quot;interesting&quot;, where in a simulationist system you can be generally comfortable in knowledge that your character is good at things they're good at, and bad at things they're bad at. So I often see silly contrivances where a master lockpick breaks his picks on a plywood door with a garbage lock because the ST didn't think it would be interesting if the characters managed to get in or out of whatever scenario the door was enforcing.<br /> <br /> Some modern systems have taken this so far as to have &quot;Story&quot; or &quot;Fate&quot; points that a player can spend to summon plot points, which I am not a fan of. My preferred style has always been that the players control their characters, the ST controls the world around them and it's reactions. Simulationism fits that best. It's not the characters against the DM or the characters against the pamphlet of very linear notes the DM has written about the story, it's the characters against the world, and the consequences of their own actions.</div> </div> </div> </div>Accidentally, you saved me the effort of writing down my opinion! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> That's exactly how I see it too. I like that simulationist games are fair, that you know where you are standing with your character, what could and couldn't you accomplish, that things are occurring along an internal consistency, not along the Rule of Cool. Good story is the ultimate goal, yes, but I like when the story is the result of the in-game events, not the other way around. Sometimes handwaving is necessary, but too much of it makes the whole thing pointless to me.<br /> <br /> Also, I don't like narrative-influencing mechanics either. Just like you, I prefer interacting with the game's world through my character only and those mechanics break immersion to me. <br /> <br /> About the &quot;playing a predetermined part&quot;, honestly, I know a lot of people like the kind of LARPs, for example, that the WW guys are organizing, like the Convention of Thorns and nothing is wrong with those, but I always felt they're closer to amateur drama classes than rpgs, even considering traditional LARPs. Although that might be exactly the case of the success of Nordic-style LARPing outside the strict rpg scene.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And while I used the terms to respond to your post, I think GNS theory is garbage. I like &quot;simulationist&quot; systems, but I also value good roleplay and don't think it's something that can be mechanically compelled. And I'm &quot;Gamist&quot; in the sense that I think every character should be equally capable of contributing to the overall goals of the group. </div> </div> </div> I agree there too. I prefer simulationist systems, but I'm not much of a powergamer type and it kinda irks me when people are saying that &quot;crunchy&quot; games are for combat-focused groups only.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-10T13:52:34-07:00","id":1090851,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1078065-why-do-you-like-simulationism?p=1090845#post1090845\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I agree there too. I prefer simulationist systems, but I'm not much of a powergamer type and it kinda irks me when people are saying that &quot;crunchy&quot; games are for combat-focused groups only.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's because all the CRUNCH is mostly combat focused with occasional utility and very far off social. So if the game has spent 50 percent of its pages on combat, 20 on other stuff, and 5 on social you're going to assume the people who play it are combat focused vs a game that was 50 percent social interactions, 30 of other actions and very little focus or crunch on combat.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-10T13:56:16-07:00","id":1090857,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1078065-why-do-you-like-simulationism?p=1090841#post1090841\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think the WOD should function on a what i call &quot;Cinematic SImulationism&quot; that is to say rather than attempting to say &quot;Its like the real world plus vampires&quot;, its easier to go &quot;Its like an action/horror/etc movie&quot; certain rules emulate tropes rather than 1 for 1 truths thus creating an emergent story that FEELS like a certain type of movie but doesn't have obvious Co-gming mechanics.<br /> <br /> Then with Degrees of Metacurrency addons depending on the group preferences with the least being Xp and the Most being Fate point style. This i think would give the best set of options all around. I don't think though any attempt at making &quot;WOD MORE REALISTIC&quot; will be anything but horrible bouts of saddness.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think I'm agreeing with you entirely. I always have problems, when an rpg says that aiming for any kind of realism in rpgs is pointless and tries to emulate, as in you example, a movie trope, or genre. My problem isn't the focusing on certain characteristics, that's entirely okay to me (otherwise, I wouldn't like WoD), but making an already unrealistic trope into the sole reference point of the game makes it cartoonish to me (but I concede, sometimes that's the exact goal). It somewhat makes it into it's own parody.<br /> <br /> Also, WoD is already sufficiently abstract to me. It has a degree of &quot;realistic simulationism&quot;, but nowhere on the level of Shadowrun, or GURPS. I'm not saying that the ST system is the holy grail oh rpgs, it has it's problems, but in my eyes it's a quite good middle ground.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, I'd agree that the ideal situation would be an edition, which is modular enough that you could make it a more &quot;realistic&quot; or a more cinematic, or narrativist game. I don't know if it is doable within one system, but it'll be cool.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-10T14:04:58-07:00","id":1090862,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1078065-why-do-you-like-simulationism?p=1090851#post1090851\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That's because all the CRUNCH is mostly combat focused with occasional utility and very far off social. So if the game has spent 50 percent of its pages on combat, 20 on other stuff, and 5 on social you're going to assume the people who play it are combat focused vs a game that was 50 percent social interactions, 30 of other actions and very little focus or crunch on combat.</div> </div> </div> </div>Okay, that's somewhat true, but mostly for D&amp;D (and derivatives) I think. Take a look, for example on Shadowrun. Yes, the combat-related crunch is robust, maybe the biggest, but there are a lot of non-combat crunch too. WoD also doesn't have as much non-combat crunch, because for most tasks, the core rules are enough. combat usually needs more rules, because it's the most &quot;dangerous&quot; thing for the characters, so a higher degree of fairness and consistency is good.<br /> <br /> Of course, if a given person or group absolutely abhors combat, then it's mostly pointless.<br /> <br /> Also there is the question of non-combat sub-systems. Some people like them, some people don't. I always felt them counterintuitive, because social interactions, for example are something done by talking and minimal amount of dice-rolling in my eyes. I have a more positive view on chasing and investigating systems, but they're often too clunky.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-10T17:10:06-07:00","id":1090929,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1078065-why-do-you-like-simulationism?p=1090857#post1090857\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I don't think I'm agreeing with you entirely. I always have problems, when an rpg says that aiming for any kind of realism in rpgs is pointless and tries to emulate, as in you example, a movie trope, or genre. My problem isn't the focusing on certain characteristics, that's entirely okay to me (otherwise, I wouldn't like WoD), but making an already unrealistic trope into the sole reference point of the game makes it cartoonish to me (but I concede, sometimes that's the exact goal). It somewhat makes it into it's own parody.<br /> <br /> Also, WoD is already sufficiently abstract to me. It has a degree of &quot;realistic simulationism&quot;, but nowhere on the level of Shadowrun, or GURPS. I'm not saying that the ST system is the holy grail oh rpgs, it has it's problems, but in my eyes it's a quite good middle ground.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, I'd agree that the ideal situation would be an edition, which is modular enough that you could make it a more &quot;realistic&quot; or a more cinematic, or narrativist game. I don't know if it is doable within one system, but it'll be cool.</div> </div> </div> </div>I have no problem with a game trying to emulate reality, CWOD has never done so, so any attempt at moving it to that level would be a massive level of change. Generally it tries to ape certain tropes, its better off pushing towards &quot;this works like a horror/action movie&quot; and thus getting the expected results of the audience. I would equally argue SHadowrun for all its crunchiness in no way tries to emulate reality, its far far more &quot;gamey&quot; than that.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1078065-why-do-you-like-simulationism?p=1090862#post1090862\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Okay, that's somewhat true, but mostly for D&amp;D (and derivatives) I think. Take a look, for example on Shadowrun. Yes, the combat-related crunch is robust, maybe the biggest, but there are a lot of non-combat crunch too. WoD also doesn't have as much non-combat crunch, because for most tasks, the core rules are enough. combat usually needs more rules, because it's the most &quot;dangerous&quot; thing for the characters, so a higher degree of fairness and consistency is good.<br /> <br /> Of course, if a given person or group absolutely abhors combat, then it's mostly pointless.<br /> <br /> Also there is the question of non-combat sub-systems. Some people like them, some people don't. I always felt them counterintuitive, because social interactions, for example are something done by talking and minimal amount of dice-rolling in my eyes. I have a more positive view on chasing and investigating systems, but they're often too clunky.</div> </div> </div> </div>If you consider the Meat of your game social interaction but don't want mechanics for it, why aren't you willing to make the sidedish of your game(combat) free form as well? If I pay for a game and don't use it I feel stupid.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-10T18:12:21-07:00","id":1090949,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1078065-why-do-you-like-simulationism?p=1090929#post1090929\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I have no problem with a game trying to emulate reality, CWOD has never done so, so any attempt at moving it to that level would be a massive level of change. Generally it tries to ape certain tropes, its better off pushing towards &quot;this works like a horror/action movie&quot; and thus getting the expected results of the audience. I would equally argue SHadowrun for all its crunchiness in no way tries to emulate reality, its far far more &quot;gamey&quot; than that.</div> </div> </div> </div>I won't try to argue for argument's sake. It might be that our point of references are different. I know a fair amount of games but I might not know the games you deem realistic. In my eyes, D&amp;D is crunchy, but not realistic, while Shadowrun is crunchy and fairly realistic, again among the games I know. No, it's not a straightforward reality simulator, it's a game, but still.<br /> <br /> WoD, in my eyes isn't really crunchy, but on that level of crunch, it's fairly realistic and that's how I love it. I wouldn't want it to turn into another SR, or an even more minimalist narrative game. I disagree that it's mainly emulating movie tropes. IMO, when stripping from the supernatural stuff, it's a quite simple universal system, which covers a failry large amount of things and situations, with the adequate level of realism for its simpleness. What in the core rules makes it unrealistic to you (again, taking into account the intended simplicity of the system)?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If you consider the Meat of your game social interaction but don't want mechanics for it, why aren't you willing to make the sidedish of your game(combat) free form as well? If I pay for a game and don't use it I feel stupid. </div> </div> </div>You misinterpreted what I said. I didn't say I don't want mechanics for social interaction, but that, for me, the core rules are enough for that, because most of the talking will be acted out, probably, since that's what a bunch of people tend to do around a table: talking. It doesn't really need elaborate rules in my eyes. Combat, in contrast, does, since you wouldn't act out maneuvers and such and even describing them is out of most people's field of knowledge. Yes you could say that not every people are eloquent, or that there are shy persons wanting to play socially active characters and elaborated social interaction subsystems help them and there is some truth to that. However, most people know how to talk, to some extent, but most of them doesn't know how to fight, but still want exciting fights in the game. Naturally, again, if you're absolutely disinterested in combat in your stories, then a minimalist combat system is sufficient. Also, as I mentioned fairness, i think combat, in general just needs more governance by the rules, since it poses a more immediate danger to the characters.<br /> <br /> Also, I'm not considering the game's Meat social interaction solely, not even in WoD. That doesn't mean I prefer combat centric games, actually I prefer a balanced approach, when there's some fight, some exploration/research and some social interaction. I don't mean equal measures in every session, but on the long run, more or less I prefer that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-10T18:26:35-07:00","id":1090951,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1078065-why-do-you-like-simulationism?p=1090929#post1090929\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I have no problem with a game trying to emulate reality, CWOD has never done so, so any attempt at moving it to that level would be a massive level of change. Generally it tries to ape certain tropes, its better off pushing towards &quot;this works like a horror/action movie&quot; and thus getting the expected results of the audience. I would equally argue SHadowrun for all its crunchiness in no way tries to emulate reality, its far far more &quot;gamey&quot; than that.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think the game should be realistic, but it should be internally consistent. I don't care if bullets blow up gas tanks as long as the setting is consistent about it. Magic exists in both settings, after all.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If you consider the Meat of your game social interaction but don't want mechanics for it, why aren't you willing to make the sidedish of your game(combat) free form as well? If I pay for a game and don't use it I feel stupid. </div> </div> </div>Probably because character reactions are something that players should have a greater degree of agency over. You don't get to choose whether or not your character is fast enough to get hit by a sword, but you should be able to choose whether or not your character is offended by a statement or afraid of someone, barring supernatural influences.<br /> <br /> Also, players tend to have a little more experience with talking to people than punching them in the face. YMMV there, I guess.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11512-zanos","name":"Zanos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11512_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"550"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-10T19:47:17-07:00","id":1090966,"title":"","content":"Yes, that is why I always tend to judge social interactions based on what my players say rather than what they roll (my players still sometimes roll the dice, but their words modify the results of the die roll). I do not like social combat, I think that social activities are best left to actual interaction rather than die rolls, and when I require dice rolls for social actions, it is because I do not want to take the time to play it out, so I do not want to take a half hour for social combat. Of course, I require description for everything that my players do (description and dice rolls are waived for routine activities unless there is a plot occurring).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-11T14:04:09-07:00","id":1091265,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Zanos</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1078065-why-do-you-like-simulationism?p=1090951#post1090951\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Probably because character reactions are something that players should have a greater degree of agency over. You don't get to choose whether or not your character is fast enough to get hit by a sword, but you should be able to choose whether or not your character is offended by a statement or afraid of someone, barring supernatural influences.<br /> <br /> Also, players tend to have a little more experience with talking to people than punching them in the face. YMMV there, I guess.</div> </div> </div> </div>Plenty of Players I know have been in fist fights, plenty of players I know who've done HEMA, SCA etc. Never met a player who's negotiated favors with a 200 year old vampire. There's a difference between talking and negotiating with a world class politician its you know why we have professional negotiators.<br /> <br /> <br /> And no there is no difference between &quot;My character doesn't get offended by being called &quot;racial slur&quot; because that's how my character is&quot; than &quot;My character doesn't get shot because he's too fast&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-11T15:49:52-07:00","id":1091313,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1078065-why-do-you-like-simulationism?p=1091265#post1091265\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Plenty of Players I know have been in fist fights, plenty of players I know who've done HEMA, SCA etc. Never met a player who's negotiated favors with a 200 year old vampire. There's a difference between talking and negotiating with a world class politician its you know why we have professional negotiators.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think you're dismissing the general situation based on the most extreme examples. I do practice HEMA. Most rpg players don't. Most of the social interactions aren't with wholly inhuman npcs, or world-class politicians, but with (mostly) average-ish people like the player. When you're out of your depth, then that's the field for the mechanics. <br /> <br /> Also, for a lot of players, rpgs are somewhat of an exercise in talking and social interaction. That, you could do around the table. Practicing a punta riversa followed by a stramazzone or a ducking+left lead combo, you couldn't.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And no there is no difference between &quot;My character doesn't get offended by being called &quot;racial slur&quot; because that's how my character is&quot; than &quot;My character doesn't get shot because he's too fast&quot; </div> </div> </div>We could agree to disagree, because I agree with Zanos that no, they aren't. The first is something immaterial, with more subtle consequences, the second is material with immediate and severe consequences. Also, physical actions need exterior arbiters, because if we're playing like children in the playground, with saying back and forth &quot;you got hit&quot; - &quot;no, I didn't&quot; that makes combat effectively meaningless and frustrating. In the meantime we apply less strict rules to the characters' inner reactions, because not getting to choose (to a reasonable extent) what your character thinks and how she reacts makes playing an rpg somewhat pointless, IMO.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-11T19:17:51-07:00","id":1091384,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1078065-why-do-you-like-simulationism?p=1091313#post1091313\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think you're dismissing the general situation based on the most extreme examples. I do practice HEMA. Most rpg players don't. Most of the social interactions aren't with wholly inhuman npcs, or world-class politicians, but with (mostly) average-ish people like the player. When you're out of your depth, then that's the field for the mechanics.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't know about your games but I generally have my characters interact with things on theirown level. My vampires deal with Vampires. My werewolves deal with werewolves, SPirits etc. I mean I guess I could use the WOD as normal people go around doing normal stuff.<br /> <br /> But more to the point I don't demand anyone taking Melee be a HEMA practioner but if someone is socially awkward but wants to play a master manipulator I'm more than willing to help them use the dice to overcome their inabiltiy to play the character.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also, for a lot of players, rpgs are somewhat of an exercise in talking and social interaction. That, you could do around the table. Practicing a punta riversa followed by a stramazzone or a ducking+left lead combo, you couldn't. </div> </div> </div>We could easily freeform out a combat, you calling off manuevers, I calling off counter manuevers until we feel things are fair. The idea that anything has to &quot;be done by dice&quot; is an arbitrary choice.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> We could agree to disagree, because I agree with Zanos that no, they aren't. The first is something immaterial, with more subtle consequences, the second is material with immediate and severe consequences. Also, physical actions need exterior arbiters, because if we're playing like children in the playground, with saying back and forth &quot;you got hit&quot; - &quot;no, I didn't&quot; that makes combat effectively meaningless and frustrating. In the meantime we apply less strict rules to the characters' inner reactions, because not getting to choose (to a reasonable extent) what your character thinks and how she reacts makes playing an rpg somewhat pointless, IMO. </div> </div> </div>Then I assume you don't play Vampire or Werewolf at all as: &quot;SOmeone insults you, roll for Frenzy&quot; is straight by the book. That's straight up taking the interaction of the character out of their hands. You even have whole clans who's thing is they don't have control of their mind that its explciitly taken away from them by the dice.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-12T09:17:52-07:00","id":1091572,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1078065-why-do-you-like-simulationism?p=1091384#post1091384\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I don't know about your games but I generally have my characters interact with things on theirown level. My vampires deal with Vampires. My werewolves deal with werewolves, SPirits etc. I mean I guess I could use the WOD as normal people go around doing normal stuff.</div> </div> </div> </div>I didn't mean that. I meant that most (conflicted) interactions and especially with new players would be with npcs like themselves. Neonates, lower rank garou, novices, etc. Not wolrd-class politics and lawyers.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But more to the point I don't demand anyone taking Melee be a HEMA practioner but if someone is socially awkward but wants to play a master manipulator I'm more than willing to help them use the dice to overcome their inabiltiy to play the character. </div> </div> </div>I agree with that. I just on the opinion that the core mechanics are enough for that and also that one of the goals of playing (to me) is practicing social skills in a safe environment, because I'm moderately socially awkward myself, so I understand that angle. Also, to me, the talking part is generally the more fluid part of the game and elaborated mechanics could break the flow of the discussion among the players and the ST. <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> We could easily freeform out a combat, you calling off manuevers, I calling off counter manuevers until we feel things are fair. The idea that anything has to &quot;be done by dice&quot; is an arbitrary choice. </div> </div> </div>I... think that the consent-based approach WW does on their LARPS is something like that, no? I'm not a big fan of it, I feel it robs the thrill and danger of the thing. In my eyes, combat needs an impartial arbiter and clear measures of abilities to be fair and interesting. I'm not saying that's the one true way, but I prefer that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Then I assume you don't play Vampire or Werewolf at all as: &quot;SOmeone insults you, roll for Frenzy&quot; is straight by the book. That's straight up taking the interaction of the character out of their hands. You even have whole clans who's thing is they don't have control of their mind that its explciitly taken away from them by the dice. </div> </div> </div>First, you don't need to be condescending to make your point. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> I play both and Brujah is my favorite clan.<br /> <br /> Second, yes those are important, but also special cases of those games. It's one of the big drawbacks of playing those monstrous creatures and part of the horror, exactly, because you lose control (and in most cases those occasions are tragic in results). But that's not the baseline.<br /> <br /> Also, I don't know about your games, but if you call a frenzy roll over every slight, slur and insult that must be an...interesting experience.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-12T12:01:37-07:00","id":1091646,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1078065-why-do-you-like-simulationism?p=1091572#post1091572\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I didn't mean that. I meant that most (conflicted) interactions and especially with new players would be with npcs like themselves. Neonates, lower rank garou, novices, etc. Not wolrd-class politics and lawyers.</div> </div> </div> </div>PLenty of world class lawyers get Embraced, and I will once again say &quot;Interactions with spirits&quot;|<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I agree with that. I just on the opinion that the core mechanics are enough for that and also that one of the goals of playing (to me) is practicing social skills in a safe environment, because I'm moderately socially awkward myself, so I understand that angle. Also, to me, the talking part is generally the more fluid part of the game and elaborated mechanics could break the flow of the discussion among the players and the ST. </div> </div> </div>I personally like the Door system from COD, but before that this is how i would do it. If its a one off thing I'd either just go with it on a simple roll if it really matter. But at important set pieces like dealing with the Primogen, or pacting with a spirit I'd generally do an extended roll. I'd give bonuses depending on the players go about it but in the end since its an important set piece like combat so I make it feel important by having the potential for failure.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I... think that the consent-based approach WW does on their LARPS is something like that, no? I'm not a big fan of it, I feel it robs the thrill and danger of the thing. In my eyes, combat needs an impartial arbiter and clear measures of abilities to be fair and interesting. I'm not saying that's the one true way, but I prefer that. </div> </div> </div>Alot of chatroom rp on the internet is purely freeform. It all comes down to consent and better descriptions, it works for sneaking, fighting etc. I am saying its purely arbitrary to decide to not engage in something mechanically and to put more mechanics on one type of engagement than others. Some game system have combat function as any other roll. No specialized methods, you win or lose in either a single roll or an extended roll.<br /> <br /> There's nothing intrinsically valuable about any method except how well it works for the group. However excessive game systems for particular types CAN push people to playing against the intended methods of the game. A game about nonviolent resolutions that has 30 pages of combat is going to have alot more combat than intended.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> First, you don't need to be condescending to make your point. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> I play both and Brujah is my favorite clan.<br /> <br /> Second, yes those are important, but also special cases of those games. It's one of the big drawbacks of playing those monstrous creatures and part of the horror, exactly, because you lose control (and in most cases those occasions are tragic in results). But that's not the baseline.<br /> <br /> Also, I don't know about your games, but if you call a frenzy roll over every slight, slur and insult that must be an...interesting experience. </div> </div> </div>V20 page 298 includes on the frenzy chart: &quot;Being harassed&quot; and &quot;Malicious Taunts&quot; so a straight by the book &quot;I'm playing a minority character vampire and some racist asshat starts saying slurs&quot; 261 in w20 doesn't include an easy slur sets you off, but being taunted by an enemy, being wounded, seeing someone you care about be wounded all takes control away from your character. <br /> <br /> And they are the baseline because they are the characters you play. I don't burst into flame in the sun or take damage from silver, or die when I get bored.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-12T16:36:36-07:00","id":1091753,"title":"","content":"I think that we are just talking about different play styles. Personally, I like simulationism for physical and mental tests, but I prefer narration for social tests. I get to be involved as the ST, which I enjoy, and my players get to be involved beyond dice throws, which they enjoy.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-13T02:54:58-07:00","id":1091878,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1078065-why-do-you-like-simulationism?p=1091753#post1091753\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think that we are just talking about different play styles. Personally, I like simulationism for physical and mental tests, but I prefer narration for social tests. I get to be involved as the ST, which I enjoy, and my players get to be involved beyond dice throws, which they enjoy.</div> </div> </div> </div>Could you elaborate?<br /> I have plenty of simulationist involvement of players 'beyond the dice throws' in social and mental situations. And as a player, social-wise, I've been in a lot of diceless social situations, but none of them have been narrative so far. (Yeah, you can tell that I haven't played indie-style games, even though I'm not dead-set on only playing traditional ones.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12622-vicky_molokh","name":"vicky_molokh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12622_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"75"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-13T05:24:25-07:00","id":1091906,"title":"","content":"Simulation requires rules and random state generation (I prefer cards and dice myself). I see narration when you use neither rules nor random state generation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-15T07:25:37-07:00","id":1092794,"title":"","content":"I've encountered cases of simulationism without use of game rules and dice (e.g. when the players study the sorts of engines available in a setting and try to extrapolate what sorts of spaceships that results in being possible, making designs etc.), and I've read some examples of narrative that relies on rules a lot, e.g. when a resisting or not resisting a flaw can drastically change the narrative outcome.<br /> <br /> Example for the latter, obviously not from WoD/ST (after all, WoD/ST is not as narrative-oriented as FATE Core):<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><br /> In a session of Hearts of Steel, Landon comes back to his home<br /> village of Vinfeld, only to find that it has been sacked by barbar-<br /> ians and that his mentor, Old Finn, has been kidnapped.<br /> Amanda tells him that the other villagers are overjoyed that<br /> he\u2019s come back, and in a scene where he talks to the village<br /> elders, she also says that they want him to stay and help with<br /> rebuilding the town.<br /> Lenny looks at some of the aspects on Landon\u2019s sheet: Disciple<br /> of the Ivory Shroud, I Owe Old Finn Everything, The Manners<br /> of a Goat, and Smashing is Always an Option. His read of those<br /> aspects is that they show Landon as being very straightforward<br /> (to the point of rudeness), aggressive, inclined to solve problems<br /> through violence, and very loyal to those he considers his own.<br /> Because of his aspects, there\u2019s not a prayer\u2019s chance in hell<br /> Landon\u2019s going to stay and help the town when Finn might still<br /> be alive. And not only that, he\u2019s going to tell the elders exactly<br /> how he feels about the fact that they didn\u2019t send a rescue party<br /> after Old Finn themselves. Probably he uses words like \u201cspine-<br /> less\u201d and \u201cworthless.\u201d You know, words that really make people<br /> sympathize with you.<br /> Amanda says that he enrages the elders so much that they\u2019re<br /> pondering banishing him from town for his insolence. She holds<br /> up a fate point and grins, indicating a compel\u2014his manners are<br /> going to get him kicked out of Vinfeld.<br /> Lenny takes it, accepting that complication. \u201cScrew them<br /> anyway,\u201d he says. \u201cI\u2019ll rescue Finn without their help.\u201d<br /> </div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12622-vicky_molokh","name":"vicky_molokh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12622_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"75"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-15T22:35:32-07:00","id":1093110,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1078065-why-do-you-like-simulationism?p=1091906#post1091906\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Simulation requires rules and random state generation (I prefer cards and dice myself). I see narration when you use neither rules nor random state generation.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> This is generally not what people mean when they say narrative. GNS theory Narrative mechanics are like Fate points, Dramatic Editing, metamechanics that allow the players to alter the setting along with the GM. Some people also think of any mechanic that attempts to push a particular genre trope vs &quot;what's realistic&quot; as narrative. This isn't the intial definition but something people use. <br /> <br /> This isn't to say you're wrong in using your term just generally that's not how narrative is used.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-16T05:56:49-07:00","id":1093197,"title":"","content":"And you generally have neither rules nor random state generation governing the effects of Fate Points, Dramatic Editing, etc. If the GM/ST allows it, it is legal, even if it is absurd (though, in its defense, reality is absurd because of black swans and dragon kings, and most people running games do not take into account the normal absurdities of life). I am not saying that narrative gaming is bad though, it is just not my preference.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-16T07:15:28-07:00","id":1093229,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1078065-why-do-you-like-simulationism?p=1093197#post1093197\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And you generally have neither rules nor random state generation governing the effects of Fate Points, Dramatic Editing, etc. If the GM/ST allows it, it is legal, even if it is absurd (though, in its defense, reality is absurd because of black swans and dragon kings, and most people running games do not take into account the normal absurdities of life). I am not saying that narrative gaming is bad though, it is just not my preference.</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually no, they are often linked to significant rules in systems that are narrative-oriented, such as classification of what counts as acceptable and what doesn't based on the impact it has on the narrative, the probability that it occurs (e.g. a Secret that complicated your life only when you roll 6 or less on 3d6 and if you don't spend a point on changing the roll result), or dramatic editing powers that have a specific cooldown and specific rules of application ('only while masked', 'only while heavy music is playing', 'only to get an opportunity to Take a Third Option' etc.). Someone with a No Good Deed Goes unpunished will be hit by narrative twists only in specific contexts, for another example.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12622-vicky_molokh","name":"vicky_molokh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12622_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"75"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-16T22:14:37-07:00","id":1093539,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1078065-why-do-you-like-simulationism?p=1093197#post1093197\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And you generally have neither rules nor random state generation governing the effects of Fate Points, Dramatic Editing, etc. If the GM/ST allows it, it is legal, even if it is absurd (though, in its defense, reality is absurd because of black swans and dragon kings, and most people running games do not take into account the normal absurdities of life). I am not saying that narrative gaming is bad though, it is just not my preference.</div> </div> </div> </div>You generally have ALOT of rules on Fate points. On the basic how much you have mechanics like &quot;How many points to change the scene by this much&quot; while this isn't random neither is how much blood I need to spend to use Protean for example. There's also simple bonuses added by Metapoints(Like +2 to a roll, or a rereoll etc) along with more explicit things like FATE's declaration system where you roll dice and then get to declare X is true(Because your character is a scientist or investigator)<br /> <br /> Nobilis is pretty damned rules complex if not heavy and doesn't have a single dice roll.<br /> <br /> Yes it comes down to the ST in the end deciding if this is stupid/crazy but that is ultimately true of player action period. That the ST says when to roll and when not to is pretty much the basis of ST games.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-18T12:52:29-07:00","id":1094271,"title":"","content":"I'm not a fan of the GNS model, as I feel it implies a certain degree of mutual exclusivity between the features it sorts into each category, which I don't think is inherent to those features. That said, what I like about games that GNS would tend to place on the more Simulationist end of the spectrum is that they tend to place heavy importance on internal consistency in the game rules, which makes it easier for players to reliably evaluate their options and make decisions with full information. This makes these games much more player-friendly than games that fall more on the Narrativist end of the spectrum. Narrativist games tend to appeal more to the same forms of engagement that GMing appeals to, which is all well and good, but the people who want that kind of engagement usually gravitate more towards being GMs than players. This often results in narrativist-style games being very popular with GMs, but their players feeling unsatisfied with the game, without fully understanding why.<br /> <br /> Ultimately, I prefer Simulationist games as a GM because they make it easier for me to create a more enjoyable experience for my players. I prefer Narrativist games as a player because they allow me to engage with the game in a more GM-like way.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":269,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","user":"Charlaquin","date":". 05-18-2017, 03:57 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-18T15:21:51-07:00","id":1094319,"title":"","content":"Interesting takes on GNS, I've often found it a rather good basic approximation in that the people I have played with's motivations are:<br /> Play a balanced &quot;game&quot;, is mechanics navigation and mastery.<br /> Explore a world/setting<br /> Tell a story<br /> <br /> People I have met tend to only like two out of the three and their reason for disliking a game often boils down to it weighing toward the third at the expense of one of their motivations. (The other reasons for not liking a game have always been either &quot;edition war&quot; stuff or just finding the games central concept/theme boring)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3326-an-fhuiseog","name":"An Fhuiseog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3326_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"271"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-18T16:25:11-07:00","id":1094336,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1078065-why-do-you-like-simulationism?p=1094271#post1094271\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm not a fan of the GNS model, as I feel it implies a certain degree of mutual exclusivity between the features it sorts into each category, which I don't think is inherent to those features. That said, what I like about games that GNS would tend to place on the more Simulationist end of the spectrum is that they tend to place heavy importance on internal consistency in the game rules, which makes it easier for players to reliably evaluate their options and make decisions with full information. This makes these games much more player-friendly than games that fall more on the Narrativist end of the spectrum. Narrativist games tend to appeal more to the same forms of engagement that GMing appeals to, which is all well and good, but the people who want that kind of engagement usually gravitate more towards being GMs than players. This often results in narrativist-style games being very popular with GMs, but their players feeling unsatisfied with the game, without fully understanding why.<br /> <br /> Ultimately, I prefer Simulationist games as a GM because they make it easier for me to create a more enjoyable experience for my players. I prefer Narrativist games as a player because they allow me to engage with the game in a more GM-like way.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Honestly I think this falls into a big falicy of equating rules Crunch with Simulationism. I can make a game about Vietnam and just use the raw statistics of survival rates roll on a chart to determine based on those statistics if the character dies. The player has no control. But its a very accurate simulation of something, probably more accurate than a heavy crunch high pc options. Conversely I can make the same game more narrative by giving them reliable hard rules for changing the odds.<br /> <br /> I think the bigger break between types of games is some people want to engage in the Meta. They want to tell a story about a character. Some people want to play a Character. Some people want to &quot;Win&quot;. SOme people want to engage in the mechanics of the system for the sake of engaging in the system. And there are degrees of how much a person values what and it changes over time.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-18T16:45:56-07:00","id":1094341,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>An Fhuiseog</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1078065-why-do-you-like-simulationism?p=1094319#post1094319\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Interesting takes on GNS, I've often found it a rather good basic approximation in that the people I have played with's motivations are:<br /> Play a balanced &amp;quot;game&amp;quot;, is mechanics navigation and mastery.<br /> Explore a world/setting<br /> Tell a story<br /> <br /> People I have met tend to only like two out of the three and their reason for disliking a game often boils down to it weighing toward the third at the expense of one of their motivations. (The other reasons for not liking a game have always been either &amp;quot;edition war&amp;quot; stuff or just finding the games central concept/theme boring)</div> </div> </div> </div>It's not a bad approximation for casual purposes, but it kind of breaks down past a certain point. I'm wary of the simulationism/narrativism split because &quot;simulationist&quot; games can and often do have a heavy focus on narrative, especially if they are focused on simulating a particular genre of fiction, and &quot;narrativist&quot; games are often quite capable of simulating believable worlds. Furthermore, game design and balance are important aspects of any game, regardless of focus on narrative or simulation.<br /> <br /> Personally, I prefer to analyze individual mechanics rather than games as a whole, and I prefer to distinguish between mechanics that cast the player in the role of actor (imagining themselves as their character and making decisions as they feel their character would), and mechanics that cast the player in the role of director (imagining themselves as co-creators in the story and making decisions for their character that they feel would improve the story). The former works best when the game mechanics are designed with the purpose of creating an internally consistent world, and the latter works best when the game mechanics empower the player to take control of the narrative, so they generally align with GNS's simulationism and narrativsim respectively. But, the actor/director model presents simulation and narration as properties of the game mechanics, instead of part of a trichotomy with game mechanics, and it puts the focus more distinctly on the type of engagement the mechanics demand of the players.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-18T16:48:14-07:00","id":1094342,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1078065-why-do-you-like-simulationism?p=1094336#post1094336\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Honestly I think this falls into a big falicy of equating rules Crunch with Simulationism. I can make a game about Vietnam and just use the raw statistics of survival rates roll on a chart to determine based on those statistics if the character dies. The player has no control. But its a very accurate simulation of something, probably more accurate than a heavy crunch high pc options. Conversely I can make the same game more narrative by giving them reliable hard rules for changing the odds.<br /> <br /> I think the bigger break between types of games is some people want to engage in the Meta. They want to tell a story about a character. Some people want to play a Character. Some people want to &amp;quot;Win&amp;quot;. SOme people want to engage in the mechanics of the system for the sake of engaging in the system. And there are degrees of how much a person values what and it changes over time.</div> </div> </div> </div>Agreed. I think there's much more value in analyzing the types of engagement a games mechanics demand and satisfy.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-18T17:20:28-07:00","id":1094349,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1078065-why-do-you-like-simulationism?p=1094336#post1094336\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think the bigger break between types of games is some people want to engage in the Meta. They want to tell a story about a character. Some people want to play a Character. Some people want to &quot;Win&quot;. SOme people want to engage in the mechanics of the system for the sake of engaging in the system. And there are degrees of how much a person values what and it changes over time.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's certainly the most important part for me. I always had a struggle to internalize the concept of &quot;telling a story about a character&quot; as a gaming style and goal. I usually want to <i>play</i> in a story, not <i>tell </i>a story as a player, because telling is something which makes distance between me and the character and the world. I just have greater degree of immersion from the standpoint of an actor or an inhabitant of the setting than from the seat of the director/god. I just like more the feeling of &quot;living&quot; a character than directing an actor's steps through a malleable play.<br /> <br /> <br /> The idea of narrative games being more popular among the GM type persons is really interesting, thanks!<br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-18T18:27:21-07:00","id":1094374,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1078065-why-do-you-like-simulationism?p=1094349#post1094349\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's certainly the most important part for me. I always had a struggle to internalize the concept of &quot;telling a story about a character&quot; as a gaming style and goal. I usually want to <i>play</i> in a story, not <i>tell </i>a story as a player, because telling is something which makes distance between me and the character and the world. I just have greater degree of immersion from the standpoint of an actor or an inhabitant of the setting than from the seat of the director/god. I just like more the feeling of &quot;living&quot; a character than directing an actor's steps through a malleable play.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think this is very common among people who prefer to be players rather than GMs in roleplaying games.<br /> <br /> I have to be really careful here, because I'm about to say something that can sound like an attack against &quot;narrativist&quot; games. But I sincerely do not mean it as such, and as I said, I prefer &quot;narrativist&quot; games when I am a player. But the thing is, the kind of engagement that narrativist or director-style mechanics demands, arguably, isn't roleplaying.<br /> <br /> To unpack that a little, I would define roleplaying as imagining yourself as another person and/or in a different situation, and behaving as you imagine you or that person would in that situation. This is the kind of engagement I think <i>most</i> people who want to participate in roleplaying games as players are <i>usually</i> looking for. And simulationist or actor-style games are specifically designed to facilitate this. They aim to present a believable world, and to provide mechanics for executing the effects of the decisions players make while imagining themselves as characters in that world. Conversely, &quot;narrativist&quot; or director-style games often ask players to control their characters from a third-party perspective, making decisions based on what they would like to happen in the story, rather than what they would imagine themselves doing in the scenario taking place. This is a perfectly valid means of engagement, and it's great that games are available that appeal to that style of engagement, but &quot;roleplaying&quot; might not be the most accurate way to describe that form of engagement. I sometimes wonder if the hobby would be better served by drawing a clearer distinction between role-playing games and collaborative storytelling games.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1078065-why-do-you-like-simulationism?p=1094349#post1094349\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The idea of narrative games being more popular among the GM type persons is really interesting, thanks!</div> </div> </div> </div>No problem! Always an interesting subject.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-18T18:51:37-07:00","id":1094382,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1078065-why-do-you-like-simulationism?p=1094374#post1094374\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think this is very common among people who prefer to be players rather than GMs in roleplaying games.<br /> <br /> I have to be really careful here, because I'm about to say something that can sound like an attack against &quot;narrativist&quot; games. But I sincerely do not mean it as such, and as I said, I prefer &quot;narrativist&quot; games when I am a player. But the thing is, the kind of engagement that narrativist or director-style mechanics demands, arguably, isn't roleplaying.<br /> <br /> To unpack that a little, I would define roleplaying as imagining yourself as another person and/or in a different situation, and behaving as you imagine you or that person would in that situation. This is the kind of engagement I think <i>most</i> people who want to participate in roleplaying games as players are <i>usually</i> looking for. And simulationist or actor-style games are specifically designed to facilitate this. They aim to present a believable world, and to provide mechanics for executing the effects of the decisions players make while imagining themselves as characters in that world. Conversely, &quot;narrativist&quot; or director-style games often ask players to control their characters from a third-party perspective, making decisions based on what they would like to happen in the story, rather than what they would imagine themselves doing in the scenario taking place. This is a perfectly valid means of engagement, and it's great that games are available that appeal to that style of engagement, but &quot;roleplaying&quot; might not be the most accurate way to describe that form of engagement. I sometimes wonder if the hobby would be better served by drawing a clearer distinction between role-playing games and collaborative storytelling games.<br /> <br /> <br /> No problem! Always an interesting subject.</div> </div> </div> </div>Really-really good points, I agree with you wholeheartedly on this! Your distinction between, strictly speaking, role playing games and collaborative storytelling games hit the nail on the head, I think and I think too, that a more clear distinction and definition would serve the hobby well.<br /> <br /> Just to be clear, I also agree that both are perfectly good ways of engagement.<br /> <br /> What do you think, that those games are seem to be more popular among GM-types could be a reason that those games are also very popular among hardcore rpg fans (most are also GMs) and developers, but not to that extent among the large swaths of (especially casual) players (though I know you don't fully agree with that evaluation, but still)?<br /> <br /> Anyway, you really added some great thoughts to the thread, which got me thinking, I greatly appreciate it (and the others' too)!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-18T19:31:12-07:00","id":1094392,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1078065-why-do-you-like-simulationism?p=1094382#post1094382\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What do you think, that those games are seem to be more popular among GM-types could be a reason that those games are also very popular among hardcore rpg fans (most are also GMs) and developers, but not to that extent among the large swaths of (especially casual) players (though I know you don't fully agree with that evaluation, but still)?</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, absolutely! Sorry, if this was what you were meaning in our earlier conversation, I misunderstood. I think this is completely spot-on, collabrative Storytelling games are very popular among GMs and developers, despite more traditional role-playing games being preferred by most players, especially casual players. I think a lot of hardcore RPG fans lose sight of the fact that the more casual fans tend to enjoy RPGs for different reasons than they do. And since the hardcore fans and GMs are usually the ones who buy the books for the group, they kind of drive the market towards their preferences.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-18T19:45:51-07:00","id":1094393,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1078065-why-do-you-like-simulationism?p=1094349#post1094349\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That's certainly the most important part for me. I always had a struggle to internalize the concept of &quot;telling a story about a character&quot; as a gaming style and goal. I usually want to <i>play</i> in a story, not <i>tell </i>a story as a player, because telling is something which makes distance between me and the character and the world. I just have greater degree of immersion from the standpoint of an actor or an inhabitant of the setting than from the seat of the director/god. I just like more the feeling of &quot;living&quot; a character than directing an actor's steps through a malleable play.<br /> <br /> <br /> The idea of narrative games being more popular among the GM type persons is really interesting, thanks!<br /> <br /> <br /></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <br /> I don't think more metagame are less like being an actor, BUT there is more a divide between method acting and being the actor who adds more to the character than was on the page. Both can give you very good and bad performances and different styles work for different people. I don't think its a matter of people who want to gm and people who don't as the breaking point but rather people who want to engage in the mechanics vs those who don't. OR narratavism is much more gamist than people think.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-18T20:17:28-07:00","id":1094398,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1078065-why-do-you-like-simulationism?p=1094393#post1094393\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't think more metagame are less like being an actor, BUT there is more a divide between method acting and being the actor who adds more to the character than was on the page. Both can give you very good and bad performances and different styles work for different people.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, actor-style and director-style aren't the most accurate ways do describe it either, but they get the idea across - in one case you imagine yourself as the character, in the other you control the character's actions as a separate agent.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1078065-why-do-you-like-simulationism?p=1094393#post1094393\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> I don't think its a matter of people who want to gm and people who don't as the breaking point but rather people who want to engage in the mechanics vs those who don't. OR narratavism is much more gamist than people think.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's certainly a factor, but there is a lot of crossover between &quot;people who want to engage with the mechanics&quot; and &quot;people who want to GM&quot;. And plenty of people who prefer more &quot;simulationist&quot; style roleplaying do indeed want to engage with the mechanics.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-19T01:33:52-07:00","id":1094457,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1078065-why-do-you-like-simulationism?p=1094341#post1094341\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's not a bad approximation for casual purposes, but it kind of breaks down past a certain point. I'm wary of the simulationism/narrativism split because &quot;simulationist&quot; games can and often do have a heavy focus on narrative, especially if they are focused on simulating a particular genre of fiction, and &quot;narrativist&quot; games are often quite capable of simulating believable worlds.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's quite true, I was trying to think are there any &quot;world simulating&quot; heavy games that actual prioritise the accuracy of the world over your ability to tell a narrative. Even a simulation heavy game like Pendragon simulates a form of literature, and hence guides story development. I can only really think of possibly H\u00e2rnMaster as a case where the world can heavily override the narrative (e.g. dungeon quests can end with you dying from slipping on a rock at the entrance)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3326-an-fhuiseog","name":"An Fhuiseog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3326_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"271"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-19T04:37:59-07:00","id":1094486,"title":"","content":"Good old HM. Very good setting, very complex game mechanics. I have converted HM to GURPS and had good results.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}